  <ros2_control name="arm_1_XArmGripperSystem" type="system">
    <hardware>
      <plugin>gz_ros2_control/GazeboSimSystem</plugin>
    </hardware>
    <joint name="arm_1_drive_joint">
      <command_interface name="position">
        </command_interface>
      <!-- The drive joint's velocity bound, and the ONLY route by which a
             declared close rate reaches the running system.

             `enforce_command_limits` clamps a POSITION command against the
             joint's velocity limit, so this pair decides how fast the gripper
             may close. There is no other way in: controller_manager 4.45.2's
             ResourceManager::import_joint_limiters passes null node interfaces,
             so no `joint_limits.<joint>.*` key in a controller configuration is
             ever read, and GripperActionController has no rate parameter of its
             own. Limits come from the description or they do not exist.

             The default is the vendor's own figure — xarm_gripper.urdf.xacro
             declares `<limit velocity="2"/>` on all six joints — rather than the
             3.14 that used to be here, which sat above that limit and was
             therefore inert. A caller that says nothing now gets what the URDF
             already says instead of a looser number that reads as a decision.

             WHY IT IS A PARAMETER. The five followers of this linkage are mimic
             joints driven by gz_ros2_control's proportional servo, whose entire
             correction authority is `follower velocity limit - leader rate`. At
             the vendor's 2 rad/s for both, that headroom is exactly zero: the
             servo saturates for the whole stroke and any perturbation is
             uncorrectable, which was the measured defect — one follower ran to
             its 0.85 limit and stayed there, leaving a pad about 23 degrees out
             of position. Bounding the leader below the follower limit is what
             gives the servo room to catch up. How far below is a fact about the
             end effector, not about this file, so it is passed in. -->
      <command_interface name="velocity">
        <param name="min">-1</param>
        <param name="max">1</param>
      </command_interface>
      <state_interface name="position"/>
      <state_interface name="velocity"/>
      <!-- <state_interface name="effort"/> -->
    </joint>
    <!-- The five follower joints of the parallel linkage.

           xarm_gripper.urdf.xacro already gives each of these a URDF
           <mimic joint="${prefix}drive_joint" multiplier="1" offset="0"/>, but a
           <mimic> tag only reaches ros2_control for a joint that also appears in
           THIS block. Without these entries HardwareInfo::mimic_joints is empty,
           and under Gazebo Harmonic nothing else couples them either: dartsim
           does not implement SetMimicConstraintFeature, and the vendor's Gazebo
           Classic mimic plugin (xarm_gripper.gazebo.xacro) cannot load. The
           measured result is a gripper with one driven finger that tilts instead
           of closing parallel, so it never touches the work-piece and never
           stalls.

           State interfaces only, and that is not a stylistic choice: ros2_control's
           component parser REJECTS a mimic joint carrying a command interface
           ("Activated mimic joints cannot have command interfaces"). A follower is
           driven by gz_ros2_control's mimic servo from the leader's position, never
           commanded independently, so it cannot be double-driven.

           CAVEAT — the multiplier is honoured, the offset is NOT. gz_ros2_control
           1.2.19 (gz_system.cpp, the mimic block in its write()) computes
           velocity_sp = -(q_follower - q_leader * multiplier) * update_rate. There
           is no offset term. Every joint below has offset="0" in the vendor URDF,
           so this is exact for THIS gripper and would be silently wrong for an end
           effector whose linkage needs one. -->
    <joint mimic="true" name="arm_1_left_finger_joint">
      <state_interface name="position"/>
      <state_interface name="velocity"/>
    </joint>
    <joint mimic="true" name="arm_1_left_inner_knuckle_joint">
      <state_interface name="position"/>
      <state_interface name="velocity"/>
    </joint>
    <joint mimic="true" name="arm_1_right_outer_knuckle_joint">
      <state_interface name="position"/>
      <state_interface name="velocity"/>
    </joint>
    <joint mimic="true" name="arm_1_right_finger_joint">
      <state_interface name="position"/>
      <state_interface name="velocity"/>
    </joint>
    <joint mimic="true" name="arm_1_right_inner_knuckle_joint">
      <state_interface name="position"/>
      <state_interface name="velocity"/>
    </joint>
  </ros2_control>
